 9060	/// Whether a declared anchor line contains anything a mutation can change
 9061	/// (M17.5 F-33 / blind pass 1 A04, A06, A10).
 9062	///
 9063	/// Operator rules already existed, but only over a mutation's before/after text
 9064	/// — i.e. only at stage 1b, where evidence exists. At 1a the packet's declared
 9065	/// anchors were checked for POSITION and never for whether the line held any
 9066	/// behaviour. Blind pass 1 walked in and found operators anchored to function
 9067	/// signatures and enum variants.
 9068	///
 9069	/// This rejects exactly one thing: a line that DECLARES rather than does. A
 9070	/// signature has no predicate to invert, no threshold to shift, no crash point
 9071	/// to disable, and no ordering to perturb; whatever the operator, the mutation
 9072	/// cannot be applied there, and a mutant that cannot be applied can never be
 9073	/// killed while still inflating the denominator §3 counts.
 9074	///
 9075	/// It deliberately does NOT try to match operators to line contents. A first
 9076	/// version did, and rejected three legitimate anchors: `NodeFlags(self.0 |
 9077	/// other.0)` is a real inversion site, `if rev == inner.state.head` is a real
 9078	/// staleness comparison, and `for entry in entries.flatten()` is a real
 9079	/// ordering site. Guessing which token an operator needs produces false
 9080	/// accusations against a mutation plan; "is this a declaration" does not.
 9081	/// Whether a mutant is GOOD is the 1b runner's question.
 9082	/// The aliases a `use` line introduces for the symbols it imports:
 9083	/// `use std::fs::{rename as mv, remove_file};` yields `("rename", "mv")`.
 9084	/// Blind-review follow-up (review of c455cdf): splitting the whole line on
 9085	/// `" as "` mangled a brace group into an alias like `mv, remove_file}`, which
 9086	/// matched nothing and reopened the evasion the alias scan closed.
 9087	/// `line` with a leading item visibility removed: `pub`, `pub(crate)`,
 9088	/// `pub(super)`, `pub(in crate::x)`. Unchanged when it carries none, and
 9089	/// unchanged for an identifier that merely starts with those letters.
 9090	fn without_visibility(line: &str) -> &str {
 9091	    let trimmed = line.trim();
 9092	    let Some(rest) = trimmed.strip_prefix("pub") else {
 9093	        return trimmed;
 9094	    };
 9095	    let rest = if let Some(open) = rest.strip_prefix('(') {
 9096	        match open.find(')') {
 9097	            Some(close) => &open[close + 1..],
 9098	            None => return trimmed,
 9099	        }
 9100	    } else {
 9101	        rest
 9102	    };
 9103	    if rest.starts_with(char::is_whitespace) {
 9104	        rest.trim_start()
 9105	    } else {
 9106	        trimmed
 9107	    }
 9108	}
